          "At its core, a **Google Chrome Extension** is a zipped bundle of standard web files—HTML, CSS, JavaScript, WebAssembly, and static assets—anchored by a single configuration blueprint: **`manifest.json`**.",
          "For over a decade, **Manifest V2 (MV2)** served as the foundation for the Chrome Web Store. However, MV2 harbored three systemic architectural flaws:",
          "**1. Persistent Memory Leaks:** In MV2, background scripts ran inside full, hidden DOM background pages (`background.scripts` or `background.page`). These pages remained resident in RAM 24/7 as long as Chrome was open, holding megabytes of memory and leaking resources even when the user wasn't interacting with them.",
          "**2. Security & Supply-Chain Vulnerabilities:** MV2 allowed developers to load remotely hosted scripts (`<script src=\"https://cdn.example.com/lib.js\">`) or execute dynamic strings via `eval()` and `new Function()`. If an attacker compromised an external CDN or database, they could silently push malicious payloads to millions of browser extensions without passing Chrome Web Store review.",
